+## Webhooks
+
+A bot can have a webhook URL, set on the bot form as "Webhook URL". It's optional, and a blank value removes it.
+When a person mentions the bot in a message, Campfire POSTs the mention to that URL. The bot can answer in the
+HTTP response, or later through the posting API.
+
+- **Trigger:** a new message from a person that mentions the bot, in a room the bot is a member of. Edits don't
+  trigger it, the same as push notifications. `Room#receive` enqueues one `Bot::WebhookJob` per mentioned bot,
+  after the message commits.
+- **Never in the request:** posting only enqueues the job. The HTTP call runs in the background (Resque in
+  production), so a slow or broken bot can't slow the poster down.
+- **Bots don't trigger bots:** a message from a bot never calls webhooks, or two bots that mention each other
+  would loop.
+- **Request:** `POST` with `Content-Type: application/json`:
+
+  ```json
+  {
+    "event":  "mention",
+    "author": { "id": 7, "name": "Alice" },
+    "room":   { "id": 5, "name": "Ops", "reply_to": "/rooms/5/<bot-code>/messages" },
+    "message": {
+      "id": 118,
+      "body": {
+        "text": "restart the build please",
+        "html": "<div>… full rich-text body …</div>"
+      },
+      "link": "/rooms/5/@118"
+    }
+  }
+  ```
+
+  - `event` is always `mention` for now. More event types may come later.
+  - `message.body.text` is the plain text with the bot's own mention removed, so it reads as the command
+    addressed to the bot. Mentions of other people stay, as `@Name`.
+  - `message.body.html` is the whole rich text body, sanitized the way it's displayed. The bot's own mention
+    stays in it.
+  - `room.reply_to` is the bot's own posting URL for that room. The bot replies out-of-band with the posting API.
+  - `message.link` opens the room scrolled to the message.
+  - Paths are relative. The bot knows its Campfire origin.
+
+### Replies
+
+| Response | Result |
+| --- | --- |
+| `200` with `Content-Type: text/plain` | The body is posted into the room as the bot's message. It's escaped, and line breaks are kept, the same as the posting API. |
+| `200` with `Content-Type: text/html` | The body is posted as rich text, sanitized before it's stored. |
+| `204` | Nothing is posted. |
+| Anything else: other statuses, other content types, redirects, timeouts | Nothing is posted. The failure is logged at warn level. |
+
+- A reply that's empty or only whitespace counts as no reply.
+- A reply over 64 KB is dropped, so a misbehaving bot can't store huge messages.
+- Redirects aren't followed, and nothing is retried, since a retry could post a second reply.
+- A reply is an ordinary message from the bot. It's broadcast and queues push notifications like any other.
+
+### Limits and safety
+
+- **Timeouts:** 5 seconds to connect, 10 seconds to read.
+- **Private addresses are refused.** The webhook's host is resolved with `RestrictedHTTP::PrivateNetworkGuard`,
+  the same guard link unfurling uses, and the connection goes to the address that was checked, so DNS can't be
+  rebound to an internal one. Loopback, private and link-local addresses are refused. A bot in development needs
+  a public hostname or a tunnel.
+- **Removed bots aren't called.** The job reloads the bot. If it has been deactivated, has left the room, or has
+  lost its webhook since the mention, nothing is sent. A message deleted before the job runs is dropped.
+
+### Mentions render through the bot's own partial
+
+ActionText renders a mention with a local named after the model: `bot` for a bot, `user` for a person. The mention
+partial, `users/_mention`, expects `user`. So bots use `bots/_mention`, which passes the bot on as `user`.
+`to_attachable_partial_path` and `to_trix_content_attachment_partial_path` both point there. Without this, a message
+that mentions a bot can't be rendered, and the message shows as unrenderable.
+
 ## Security notes
 
 The bot code is a credential. It's stored in plaintext, because the bots page has to show it again later.
 Given that:
 
 - **Visibility:** only the creator and administrators see a bot's code. It's shown on the bots list only,
-  not on the edit page. Bots have no JSON representation.
-- **Caching:** the bots index responds with `Cache-Control: no-store`, and carries
-  `<meta name="turbo-cache-control" content="no-cache">` so Turbo doesn't keep a snapshot of it.
+  not on the edit page. Bots have no JSON representation. The edit page shows the webhook URL instead.
+- **Caching:** every page that shows a bot's code or webhook URL (the bots index, and the new and edit pages)
+  responds with `Cache-Control: no-store` and carries `<meta name="turbo-cache-control" content="no-cache">`,
+  so neither browsers nor Turbo keep a copy.
+- **Webhook URLs can be secrets too.** Many services put a token in the path or query. They're treated like
+  bot codes: shown only to the creator and administrators, never cached, and masked in logs (`:webhook` in
+  `filter_parameters`). Failure logs carry the bot and message ids, never the URL or the body.
 - **Logs:** Rails request logs and parameter logs mask the code (see above).
 - **Known gap, SQL logs:** the development log includes bot codes inline in SQL statements (`WHERE bot_key = '…'`).
   Session tokens and password digests already appear the same way, so this follows the existing posture. Masking
@@ -107,7 +185,16 @@ Given that:
 - **Bot messages can only be edited or deleted by administrators,** not the bot's creator, since authorship
   is the bot. Widen `Current.user.can_administer?(message)` if creators should be able to moderate their bots.
 - **No rate limiting** on the posting endpoint. A leaked code can post freely until it's deleted.
-- **No size limit:** nothing caps the message size beyond the server's own request limits.
+- **No size limit:** nothing caps the message size on the posting API beyond the server's own request limits.
+  Webhook replies are capped at 64 KB.
+- **Webhook requests aren't signed.** A bot can't verify that a call came from Campfire. An unguessable path in
+  the webhook URL is the only check for now. An HMAC header would need a per-bot secret.
+- **No delivery log or retries.** A failed call only shows in the server log, so a bot's author can't see why a
+  reply didn't appear.
+- **Replies keep mention attachments.** An HTML reply can mention people the way a person's message can, so a bot
+  can ping room members. Plain text replies never ping anyone.
+- **No rate limiting on webhook calls.** Each mention calls the webhook, and nothing throttles that.
+- **Event types:** only `mention` exists.
 
